  <!-- Bag Types -->
  <h2 id="bag-types">Backpack vs Messenger vs Briefcase: Which is Best?</h2>
  <p>
    Each bag type has strengths for different situations:
  </p>

  <h3>Backpack (Recommended for Travel)</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> Airports, public transit, walking cities, all-day carry
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Distributes weight evenly across both shoulders</li>
    <li>Hands-free mobility</li>
    <li>Higher capacity options available</li>
    <li>Most comfortable for extended wear</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Downside:</strong> Less professional appearance in some contexts; harder to quickly grab items
  </p>

  <h3>Messenger Bag</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> Short commutes, client meetings, cafes
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Quick access to contents</li>
    <li>More professional look than backpacks</li>
    <li>Easy to swing around and access while moving</li>
    <li>Often has more pockets for small items</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Downside:</strong> Shoulder strain over long distances; less ergonomic for heavy loads
  </p>

  <h3>Briefcase</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> Business travel, formal meetings, corporate environments
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Most professional appearance</li>
    <li>Structured protection for documents and laptop</li>
    <li>Clear separation of compartments</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Downside:</strong> Least comfortable to carry; ties up one hand; lowest capacity
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Our recommendation:</strong> Get a quality backpack for travel (like those reviewed above), then add a
    packable messenger or sleeve for client-facing situations.
  </p>

  <!-- TSA Features -->
  <h2 id="tsa-features">TSA-Friendly Features to Look For</h2>
  <p>
    If you fly frequently, TSA-friendly design saves time and frustration:
  </p>

  <h3>Checkpoint-Friendly Design</h3>
  <p>
    These bags let you leave your laptop inside during X-ray screening. They have laptop compartments that can be
    scanned separately from other contents:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Clamshell opening:</strong> Opens fully flat like a suitcase (Nomatic, Tortuga)</li>
    <li><strong>Butterfly design:</strong> Laptop section unfolds independently (some Aer models)</li>
    <li><strong>Dedicated laptop zip:</strong> Separate zipper accesses only laptop (Aer Travel Pack 3)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What Makes a Bag "TSA-Friendly"</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Laptop compartment opens independently from main compartment</li>
    <li>No metal, liquids, or dense items sharing the laptop section</li>
    <li>Lies flat on the X-ray belt</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <em>Note: Even with checkpoint-friendly bags, TSA agents may occasionally ask you to remove your laptop.
      Design helps but doesn't guarantee hands-off screening.</em>
  </p>

  <!-- Anti-Theft -->
  <h2 id="anti-theft">Anti-Theft Features Worth Having</h2>
  <p>
    Security concerns are real for nomads. Here are features that actually help:
  </p>

  <h3>Lockable Zippers</h3>
  <p>
    Most premium bags (Nomatic, Aer, Tortuga) have zipper pulls that accept small TSA locks. This deters
    opportunistic theft and keeps contents secure in hostel storage.
  </p>

  <h3>Hidden Pockets</h3>
  <p>
    Back-panel pockets (against your body) keep valuables like passport and cash out of sight. Bellroy and
    Pacsafe excel here.
  </p>

  <h3>RFID Blocking</h3>
  <p>
    Some bags include RFID-blocking pockets for cards and passports. Nice to have, but a simple RFID-blocking
    card sleeve works just as well for $10.
  </p>

  <h3>Cut-Resistant Materials</h3>
  <p>
    Pacsafe Venturesafe bags have slash-proof panels and straps. Useful in high-theft destinations, but adds
    weight and cost.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Reality check:</strong> Most theft is opportunistic—don't leave bags unattended, use hotel safes, and
    be aware in crowds. A lockable main compartment and common sense prevent 95% of issues.
  </p>

